The signature is HMAC-SHA256, keyed with the webhook's secret ( whsec_… , shown once when the webhook is created), over the string "<t>.<raw request body>" . Verify against the raw bytes you received — never a re-serialised object — and reject timestamps more than five minutes old.
// Node 18+ / Deno / Workers
async function verify(secret: string, header: string, rawBody: string) {
const parts = Object.fromEntries(header.split(",").map((p) => p.split("=", 2)));
const t = Number(parts.t);
if (Math.abs(Date.now() / 1000 - t) > 300) return false;
const key = await crypto.subtle.importKey("raw", new TextEncoder().encode(secret),
{ name: "HMAC", hash: "SHA-256" }, false, ["sign"]);
const mac = await crypto.subtle.sign("HMAC", key, new TextEncoder().encode(`${t}.${rawBody}`));
const hex = [...new Uint8Array(mac)].map((b) => b.toString(16).padStart(2, "0")).join("");
return timingSafeEqual(hex, parts.v1);
}
The same function, tested against the server's signing, is in src/lib/org/webhookSignature.ts .

Source: https://www.noshashi.app/docs/webhooks/

There are no token allowances on the XRP Ledger, but some things you leave open let others take value later:
a check you wrote (the payee can pull up to its amount);
an NFT sell offer (anyone, or its named buyer, can take the NFT at that price; at zero it is a giveaway);
a funded payment channel;
an old DEX order;
a regular key or signer you no longer control.
Each can be cancelled with one transaction.

Source: https://www.noshashi.app/learn/

A phishing site does not need your seed if you will sign for it. The signatures that empty accounts are:
SetRegularKey or SignerListSet , which hand signing power to someone else;
AccountDelete , which sends everything to its Destination;
an NFTokenCreateOffer selling an NFT for nothing.
Decode every request before signing it.

Source: https://www.noshashi.app/learn/

Reserves alone prove little: a business can show a large balance and owe more. The other half is liabilities , what it owes its customers. A Merkle sum tree proves that total without revealing anyone's balance:
each customer's balance becomes a leaf, keyed by a hash nobody else can link to them;
pairs of leaves are hashed together with their sums, up to a single root and a total;
the institution publishes only the root, the total and the number of customers;
each customer receives the path from their leaf to the root and can check that their balance is counted, and that no branch carries a negative sum (the trick that hides debts).
Reserves divided by that total is the coverage ratio . NOSHASHI reads the named reserve accounts from a validated ledger every day and records an attestation, each one hash-chained to the one before, so a changed record shows.
REAL NUMBERS, RECORDED FROM MAINNET TODAY
The same tree built over the 138 holders of a real issued token (USDB) totals exactly what the ledger itself reports the issuer owes: 33,733,671.32 USDB. Understate one holder's balance and their path no longer reaches the root.

Source: https://www.noshashi.app/learn/
